// F1 Help (HLPWIND.PAS). Real Pascal reads this from a compiled binary help file (ANACREON.HLP -- a
// FILE OF HelpPage, HelpPage = ARRAY[1..19] OF STRING[80]) that isn't part of this repo -- the pages
// below are that file's own text, decoded once (14 pages, confirmed against the real file's exact
// byte length: 21546 = 1539 * 14, where 1539 = 19 lines * 81 bytes per length-prefixed STRING[80])
// and transcribed here, not shipped as a binary asset. Numbered 1-14 in physical record order (the
// order they were decoded in); `page N` below is the (N)th physical record, 1-based.
//
// `index` mirrors GetIndexEntry's own 15-line menu (HLPWIND.PAS:133-149) verbatim, including its own
// dotted-leader label text and the two pairs of topics that intentionally share one page (Function
// Keys/Windows -> page 3, Materials/Raw Materials -> page 8, Ships/Defenses -> page 7) -- but each
// entry's own `page` property here is IndexPageNo's real value (HLPWIND.PAS:46-49) PLUS ONE, not the
// literal array value: Turbo Pascal's Seek(HelpFile, n) is 0-based ("the first component has value
// 0"), so a PageNo of 6 (Defenses' real IndexPageNo entry) actually seeks to the *7th* physical
// record -- confirmed the hard way, by first transcribing IndexPageNo's literal values here and
// finding Defenses landed on the Technology page instead of Ships and Defenses when driven live.
// InitializeHelpWindow's own PageNo:=1 default is the same story: it opens on the *2nd* physical
// record ("Help!", the quick-start page) as this window's real first screen, not the 1st ("Index", a
// microscopic cursor-instructions blurb that turns out to be unreachable through any real navigation
// path at all -- PageDown's own guard floors PageNo at 1, so PageNo can never become 0 and Seek can
// never reach record 0 in play). Kept here anyway, as page 1, for provenance -- it's real decoded
// content from the shipped file, just dead code as far as the original game went; HelpWindow's own
// navigation floor starts at page 2 to match.
//
// One correction to the source file's own content: its page 2 ("Status Windows") lists a separate
// <F2> Display Window / <F4> Military Status / <F6> Fleet Status -- stale by the time 1.31 shipped.
// SWINDOWS.PAS's own live help-line string (the actual in-game hint bar, drawn every turn, confirmed
// at SWINDOWS.PAS:245/253/261) reads exactly "F1:Help F3:Status F5:Fleets F7:News F8:Empire F9:Names
// F10:Map" -- no F2/F4/F6 at all, World and Military status already merged into F3, Fleet position
// and composition already merged into F5. Corrected here to match that authoritative, load-bearing
// string rather than the unmaintained help text (everything else on every other page is transcribed
// as-is, typos included).

help {
    index {
        entry "Combat............................11" page=12
        entry "Construction......................10" page=11
        entry "Defenses...........................6" page=7
        entry "Fleet Orders......................12" page=13
        entry "Function Keys......................2" page=3
        entry "ISSP..............................13" page=14
        entry "Materials..........................7" page=8
        entry "Raw Materials......................7" page=8
        entry "Ships..............................6" page=7
        entry "Technology Levels..................5" page=6
        entry "Windows............................2" page=3
        entry "   Map.............................8" page=9
        entry "   World Status....................9" page=10
        entry "World Classes......................4" page=5
        entry "World Types......................3" page=4
    }

    // Page 1 -- the index menu's own legend, and (PageNo:=1 in InitializeHelpWindow) the very first
    // screen shown when Help opens, before any navigation.
    page 1 {
        text #"""
 Index

     ,   Moves the cursor.
 <Enter>   Selects an entry.
   <Esc>   Exits the index.
"""#
    }

    // Page 2 -- "Help!"
    page 2 {
        text #"""
 Help!

 The on-line screens have charts and tables that you might need while
 playing. Use the <PgUp> and <PgDn> keys to move through the screens. Press
 the <End> key to see the index.

 If you have NOT read the manual, here are some quick hints to play the game:

 ■ You can refer to planets by their coordinates. The coordinate system is
   cartessian (positive x to the right, positive y towards the top) and
   centered on your capital.

 ■ You must deploy fleets to attack worlds or move ships about. Fleets do not
   move until you end your turn.

 ■ To get back to the main menu, end your turn with <Game/Next Turn>. When
   the computer asks for your password, press <Esc>.
"""#
    }

    // Page 3 -- "Status Windows" (Function Keys / Windows index entries). F-key list corrected --
    // see this file's own header comment.
    page 3 {
        text #"""
 Status Windows                                                  Page 2

  <F1>        Help Window.
  <F3>        Status.                World and military status: class, type,
                                      tech level, materials, ships, defenses.
  <F5>        Fleets.                Position, destination, and composition.
  <F7>        News.
  <F8>        Empire Status.         Industrial and military totals.
  <F9>        Names.                 Currently defined names.
  <F10>       Map.                   Map of the galaxy.

  <Esc>       Exit a window.

 Windows are very useful because they provide the emperor or empress with
 instant information from any point in the program.
"""#
    }

    // Page 4 -- "World Types"
    page 4 {
        text #"""
 World Types                                                     Page 3

  a     agricultural            sup
  A     ambrosia                amb            1 and A class worlds only
  b     base planet             ships          produce LAMs as well
  C     capital                 ships          center of the empire
  c     chemical                che
  i     independent             ships          non-aligned worlds
  j     jumpship base           hkr,jmp,jtn
  m     metal mining            met
  N     ninja base              amb,nnj        need ambrosia
  o     outpost                 -              starbase designation
  r     raw material mine       che,met,tri
  s     starship base           str,pen
  t     transport base          fgt,trn
  U     research university     -              helps empire increase tech
  z     trillum mine            tri
"""#
    }

    // Page 5 -- "World Classes"
    page 5 {
        text #"""
 World Classes                                                   Page 4

  0  artificial     (starbase)           j  class j       (standard)
  1  paradise       (good for all)       k  class k       (standard)
  2  ruins          (ancient tech)       l  class l       (standard)
  A  ambrosia       (ambrosia)           m  class m       (standard)
  a  arid           (dry, need sup)      O  ocean         (poor for mining)
  B  barren         (air-less)           P  poisonous     (good for che)
  D  desert         (good for tri)       U  underground   (good for mining)
  E  earth-like     (standard)           V  volcanic      (good for mining)
  F  forest         (good for sup)
  G  gas giant      (good for che)
  h  hostile life   (alien life)
  I  ice world      (poor for all)
  J  jungle         (good for sup)

 The class of a world is an indication of its natural environment.  Different
 environments affect industries differently.  E.g. ocean worlds are good for
 the production of supplies, but poor for metal mining.
"""#
    }

    // Page 6 -- "Technology"
    page 6 {
        text #"""
 Technology                                                      Page 5

  pt    pre-technological    sup
  p     primitive            men, met
  pa    pre-atomic           che
  a     atomic               GDM, tri
  pw    pre-warp             fgt
  w     warp                 trn
  j     jump                 jmp, jtn, ion
  b     bio-technology       hkr, pen, def, amb, OUTPOST
  s     starship             str, nnj, SRM, COMPLEX, COMMAND, LAM
  pg    pre-gate             frt, LINK, DISRUPTOR
  g     gate                 GATE

 Attaining a given tech level is a pre-requisite to attaining any of its
 technologies.  All technologies for a given level must be acquired before
 the next tech level can be reached.  For example, a capital must be bio-tech
 level before it can acquire hunter-killers, but ALL the technologies must be
 acquired before the capital can reach starship level.
"""#
    }

    // Page 7 -- "Ships and Defenses"
    page 7 {
        text #"""
 Ships and Defenses                                              Page 6

 Ships                Drv    Arms/Armor   Tech  Notes
  fgt fighters        Wrp   light/none     pw
  hkr hunter-killers  Jmp  medium/light     b   stealth/cloaking
  jmp jumpships       Jmp   light/light     j
  jtn jumptransports  Jmp    none/light     j
  pen penetrators     Wrp2 medium/medium    b   stealth/warp 2 sectors
  str starships       Wrp   heavy/heavy     s
  trn transports      Wrp    none/none      w

 Defenses                    Arms/Armor   Tech  Notes
  def defense satellites    heavy/heavy     b
  GDM ground defense       medium/none      a
  ion ion cannons          medium/medium    j
  LAM attack missiles       heavy/none      s   only base planets build these
  men GAT, troops             ---/---       p
  nnj ninja                   ---/---       s   very powerful against men
"""#
    }

    // Page 8 -- "Materials"
    page 8 {
        text #"""
 Materials                                                       Page 7

 Materials        Units  Tech  Notes

  amb ambrosia    KTons    b   a drug that increases worker productivity.
  che chemicals   MTons   pa   used to build ships and make ambrosia.
  met metals      MTons    p   used to build ships and industry.
  sup supplies    MTons   pt   food, medicine, etc.
  tri trillum     KTons    a   used to build and power ships.

 Any world that has ambrosia will run the risk of addicting its population.
 Once the population is addicted, workers will be more efficient, but there-
 after any interruption in their supply will cause widespread riots.

 Trillum is needed on all worlds that deploy fleets.  The amount will vary
 depending on the size and composition of the fleet, but you should try to
 keep at least 200 Ktons on all your worlds.
"""#
    }

    // Page 9 -- "Map Window"
    page 9 {
        text #"""
 Map Window                                                      Page 8

 ─┼─ ·  ·  ·  · ─┼─░░░░░░░░░░░░─┼─          #  Construction Site
  ·  p Unknown   ·    ░░░░░░░░░░░░          +  SRMs
  ·     World    · ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░          π  Industrial Complex
  ·              · ░░Dark Nebula░░          ■  Command Base
  ·   Fleet      ·    ░░░░░░░░░░░░          ≡  Fortress
 ─┼─ · · ·  · ─C─ ·  · ░░░░░░░░░
  ·      Enemy Fleet         ▒▒▒▒▒            Warp Link
  ·              ·    ▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒            Gate
  ·              · ▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒
  ·          ▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒Nebula▒▒▒
 ─┼─ ·  ·  ·  · ─┼─ · ▒▒▒▒▒▒ · ─┼─

 The scan window displays the positions of all the planets in the galaxy.  If
 the world has been scouted by the empire, then the letter designating its
 type will appear on the map, otherwise the letter 'p' will show its
 position.  Other symbols are identified above.

"""#
    }

    // Page 10 -- "World Status Window"
    page 10 {
        text #"""
 World Status Window                                             Page 9

 Name or coordinates       Ambrosia
 of the world               addiction
     │                        │          Revolution
     │   Empire to which      │           index         Raw materials
     │   it belongs           │            │             on the world
     │     │                  │            │                │
 PlntName Sta C T Tl  Pop Eff A Impt Expt Rev  jtn  trn  amb..tri
              │ │  │   │   │      └─┬─┘         │    │
              │ │  │   │   │     ISSP setting   │    │
              │ │  │   │   │                    │    │
              │ │  │   │  Efficiency (%)        │   Number of transports
              │ │  │  Population (in billions)  │       on the world
              │ │  Technology Level             │
              │ World Type                     Number of jumptransports
         World Class                               on the world
"""#
    }

    // Page 11 -- "Construction"
    page 11 {
        text #"""
 Construction                                                    Page 10

 Construction         Tech   che/yr  met/yr  tri/yr   Years

  command base           s      460    2300    180       6
  industrial complex     s      590    2600    150      10
  disruptor             pg     1100    1180   1120      10
  fortress              pg      840    2870    250      12
  stargate               g     2530    3920   1450      15
  outpost                b      350    1120    150       3
  mines                  s      110     500     80       2
  warp link             pg     1560    2550    290       5

 Every year you must supply the construction site with the necessary raw
 materials.  For example, if you're building a stargate, every year you must
 have a fleet at the site with at least 2530 megatons of chemicals, 3920
 megatons of metals, and 1450 kilotons of trillum.
"""#
    }

    // Page 12 -- "Combat"
    page 12 {
        text #"""
 Combat                                                          Page 11

 The best way to learn how to attack is to read the manual, but if your must
 try right away, follow these guidelines:

 ■ First, target all your groups with the <T>arget command. Pressing <-> for
   a target means that the group is defending.

 ■ If there are enemy ships in the same orbit with you, press <E>ngage to
   carry out the attack.  If there are no ships opposing you, press <M>ove to
   advance to the next orbit.

 ■ In the <M>ove command, the options for each group are: <S>tay, <R>etreat,
   or <A>dvance.

 ■ Once your transports have landed on the ground, press <E>ngage until
   the enemy surrenders, or all your men are killed.
"""#
    }

    // Page 13 -- "Fleet Orders"
    page 13 {
        text #"""
 Fleet Orders                                                    Page 12

 Fleets may be given the following orders:

 DESTination <xy>:  Changes the destination of the fleet to the given
 coordinate or name.  For example: "DEST Home" or "DEST 0,7".

 REPEat:  Repeats the sequence of commands from the beginning until
 the player cancels the orders. If no repeat command if found at the end,
 the fleet forgets its orders when it is done.

 TRANsfer <amt> <res>:  Transfers the given number of materials of the
 given resource to or from the planet. If the number is positive, the
 materials are transferred to the fleet. If the number is negative,
 the materials are transferred to the world. For example, "TRAN 1000 met"
 moves 1000 megatons of metals to the fleet.

 WAIT:  Waits at the current destination for one year.
"""#
    }

    // Page 14 -- "Industrial Self Sufficiency Percentage"
    page 14 {
        text #"""
 Industrial Self Sufficiency Percentage                          Page 13

 Changing the ISSP of a world is one way in which an empire can increase
 the production of ships. Normally a world devotes a portion of its industry
 to produce the chemicals, metals, and trillum needed to build ships.
 By lowering the ISSP for each of these raw materials, an empire can
 instruct a world to concentrate more industry on its shipyards.

 For example, a base planet normally uses 50% of its industrial capacity
 to build ships, 15% to mine metals, 10% to mine trillum, 10% to produce
 chemicals, and 15% to produce supplies. By lowering the ISSP for
 metals, the world can be instructed to produce fewer metals and instead
 concentrate its industry on building ships. Although the world will build
 more ships, the emperor or empress must insure that metals are brought in
 from other worlds.
"""#
    }
}
